1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,750
I reached the only road into
town one minute after it closed.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,190
The road was a tunnel.

3
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:06,690
The tunnel had gone to bed.

4
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:08,310
I was holding the timetable.

5
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:09,530
I had not read it.

6
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:10,570
Hi, I'm Wob.

7
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:13,150
I visit odd places and
ask why anyone stays.

8
00:00:14,728 --> 00:00:17,298
So I slept in the car, with the roof down,

9
00:00:17,448 --> 00:00:19,538
because I had not
figured out the roof yet.

10
00:00:19,748 --> 00:00:22,118
On the other side of that
mountain is Whittier,

11
00:00:22,288 --> 00:00:23,088
Alaska,

12
00:00:23,288 --> 00:00:25,578
a small port on a long grey inlet.

13
00:00:25,888 --> 00:00:28,418
Most of the people who live
there share one front door.

14
00:00:28,668 --> 00:00:30,498
One building, fourteen floors.

15
00:00:30,908 --> 00:00:32,138
I came here to move in.

16
00:00:32,308 --> 00:00:33,478
By morning I had questions.

17
00:00:33,868 --> 00:00:36,738
Why build a town behind a
mountain that closes at night?

18
00:00:36,908 --> 00:00:39,398
And why would almost everyone
end up in the same building?

19
00:00:39,448 --> 00:00:42,718
Before this trip was
over, I had both answers,

20
00:00:42,948 --> 00:00:45,738
and I changed my mind about
living here more than once.

21
00:00:47,672 --> 00:00:51,234
In the morning I joined the queue at
the staging area and finally read the

22
00:00:51,284 --> 00:00:52,172
timetable.

23
00:00:52,335 --> 00:00:55,360
In winter, the tunnel opens
at seven in the morning,

24
00:00:55,410 --> 00:00:57,434
and the gate locks at eleven at night.

25
00:00:57,597 --> 00:01:01,309
Cars and trains take turns,
in fifteen-minute windows,

26
00:01:01,359 --> 00:01:02,897
one direction at a time.

27
00:01:03,072 --> 00:01:05,547
Picture a one-lane bridge
with a traffic light.

28
00:01:05,709 --> 00:01:08,735
Now make the bridge about
two and a half miles long,

29
00:01:08,797 --> 00:01:11,684
roughly four kilometres,
and let a train borrow it.

30
00:01:11,847 --> 00:01:12,922
A train borrowed it.

31
00:01:13,084 --> 00:01:15,797
My window slid past while
I counted carriages.

32
00:01:15,959 --> 00:01:19,272
Then the next window opened, for
traffic coming the other way.

33
00:01:19,447 --> 00:01:20,334
I waved at them.

34
00:01:20,510 --> 00:01:21,747
They did not wave back.

35
00:01:21,922 --> 00:01:23,935
They were busy reading their timetables.

36
00:01:24,110 --> 00:01:28,410
The timetable says unscheduled trains
can hold things up by fifteen to thirty

37
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:29,260
minutes.

38
00:01:29,347 --> 00:01:31,397
It also had a note about cars like mine.

39
00:01:32,816 --> 00:01:34,354
My rental was a convertible.

40
00:01:34,516 --> 00:01:37,429
Convertibles have to close
their roofs before going in,

41
00:01:37,554 --> 00:01:40,054
so I wrestled mine up while
the whole queue watched.

42
00:01:40,229 --> 00:01:41,541
Then there is the cold rule.

43
00:01:41,716 --> 00:01:44,103
When it is thirty-two Fahrenheit or below,

44
00:01:44,166 --> 00:01:48,741
that's zero Celsius, traffic only goes
in during the last five minutes of each

45
00:01:48,791 --> 00:01:49,591
window.

46
00:01:49,728 --> 00:01:51,528
Finally the light turned green.

47
00:01:51,691 --> 00:01:55,928
Inside, the road and the railway
share the same strip of concrete,

48
00:01:55,978 --> 00:01:58,166
with the rails sunk right into the lane.

49
00:01:58,329 --> 00:02:02,216
It is the longest combined rail and
highway tunnel in North America,

50
00:02:02,278 --> 00:02:05,291
and cars have only been allowed
through since two thousand.

51
00:02:05,466 --> 00:02:09,016
I kept both hands on the wheel and
did not breathe until daylight.

52
00:02:09,178 --> 00:02:10,466
Rock on both sides.

53
00:02:10,629 --> 00:02:11,966
Rails under my tyres.

54
00:02:12,129 --> 00:02:14,166
Somewhere behind me, my courage.

55
00:02:15,871 --> 00:02:19,709
Out the far side, the clouds sat
so low I could have worn one.

56
00:02:19,872 --> 00:02:20,797
Grey water.

57
00:02:20,959 --> 00:02:21,821
Waterfalls.

58
00:02:21,984 --> 00:02:24,022
Mountains going straight
up out of the sea.

59
00:02:24,196 --> 00:02:25,422
So why put a town here?

60
00:02:25,584 --> 00:02:26,634
My guess was gold.

61
00:02:26,796 --> 00:02:29,571
I was not completely wrong
about people passing through.

62
00:02:29,759 --> 00:02:33,696
For thousands of years the
Chugach, an Alutiiq people,

63
00:02:33,746 --> 00:02:38,709
crossed Portage Pass behind this inlet to
trade with Dena'ina people on Cook Inlet.

64
00:02:38,871 --> 00:02:43,722
In the late eighteen hundreds, gold
seekers hauled sleds over the same pass.

65
00:02:43,897 --> 00:02:46,959
But hauling sleds is not the
same as building a tower.

66
00:02:47,109 --> 00:02:48,709
Gold did not build this town.

67
00:02:48,884 --> 00:02:49,684
Weather did.

68
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:53,420
Turns out the fog was a selling point.

69
00:02:53,582 --> 00:02:58,183
During the Second World War, the US
Army wanted a supply port in Alaska,

70
00:02:58,233 --> 00:03:00,370
and this inlet had a lot going for it.

71
00:03:00,545 --> 00:03:04,520
The water is deep and does not freeze,
so ships could come in all year.

72
00:03:04,683 --> 00:03:06,645
According to the state's tunnel history,

73
00:03:06,695 --> 00:03:10,107
going on to Seward added about
fifty-two miles of sailing.

74
00:03:10,270 --> 00:03:14,395
And the low cloud and fog helped
hide the port from Japanese planes.

75
00:03:14,595 --> 00:03:18,145
From the air, the fog was a
blanket pulled over the harbour.

76
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,632
To connect the port to the rest of Alaska,

77
00:03:20,682 --> 00:03:23,370
the Army built a railway
through two mountains,

78
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:25,407
finished on April the twenty-third,

79
00:03:25,457 --> 00:03:26,645
nineteen forty-three.

80
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:29,695
The longer tunnel is named
after Anton Anderson,

81
00:03:29,745 --> 00:03:31,582
the Army engineer in charge.

82
00:03:31,745 --> 00:03:36,120
By the mid nineteen forties, more than a
thousand soldiers and civilians lived here.

83
00:03:36,295 --> 00:03:37,795
I put my sunglasses away.

84
00:03:37,957 --> 00:03:39,408
I also forgave the tunnel.

85
00:03:39,570 --> 00:03:41,208
It was never built for my car.

86
00:03:43,445 --> 00:03:46,519
Back home, I unfold the
timetable on my desk,

87
00:03:46,569 --> 00:03:49,407
because this is the point
where my trip went indoors.

88
00:03:49,569 --> 00:03:53,795
After the war, the Army put up
two huge buildings in Whittier.

89
00:03:53,969 --> 00:03:56,270
One of them is why my
mail had a floor number.

90
00:03:56,445 --> 00:03:59,445
The Hodge Building, now
called Begich Towers,

91
00:03:59,495 --> 00:04:01,857
is fourteen floors of pale concrete,

92
00:04:01,907 --> 00:04:05,907
finished in nineteen fifty-seven
after going up in three sections.

93
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:10,082
According to local news, nearly
everyone in town lives inside it.

94
00:04:10,257 --> 00:04:13,820
The twenty twenty census counted
about two hundred and seventy people,

95
00:04:13,945 --> 00:04:16,169
and estimates vary a little since then.

96
00:04:17,597 --> 00:04:20,272
So I spent one winter day
without going outside.

97
00:04:20,434 --> 00:04:23,221
Morning: I went down for
my mail in my slippers.

98
00:04:23,396 --> 00:04:25,496
The post office is inside the building,

99
00:04:25,546 --> 00:04:26,934
close to the entrance.

100
00:04:27,097 --> 00:04:28,447
So is the police station.

101
00:04:28,609 --> 00:04:32,372
Late morning: the clinic, then
the convenience store for snacks.

102
00:04:32,534 --> 00:04:37,447
Afternoon: laundry, still indoors,
with my basket riding the elevator.

103
00:04:37,621 --> 00:04:41,534
There is a church in here too,
plus city offices and a playground.

104
00:04:41,746 --> 00:04:45,584
Think of an apartment block where the
whole town has moved into the lobby.

105
00:04:45,746 --> 00:04:46,546
And the school?

106
00:04:46,696 --> 00:04:50,484
It sits across the road, and children
get there through a tunnel underneath.

107
00:04:50,647 --> 00:04:52,497
My boots never left the closet.

108
00:04:52,672 --> 00:04:53,784
They seemed relieved.

109
00:04:55,554 --> 00:04:58,253
From the lobby window I
could see the other building.

110
00:04:58,416 --> 00:04:59,591
Nobody lives in it.

111
00:04:59,754 --> 00:05:02,916
The Buckner Building was finished
in the early nineteen fifties,

112
00:05:02,966 --> 00:05:07,516
according to old Army records, with six
floors for about a thousand soldiers.

113
00:05:07,678 --> 00:05:11,741
Inside were a movie theatre, a
bowling alley and a small clinic.

114
00:05:11,916 --> 00:05:15,041
Before my tower, this was
the town under one roof,

115
00:05:15,104 --> 00:05:16,978
and it did not stay full for long.

116
00:05:17,154 --> 00:05:19,966
The Army began pulling
out around nineteen sixty,

117
00:05:20,029 --> 00:05:23,016
and records differ on when
the last soldiers left.

118
00:05:23,178 --> 00:05:24,816
Today Buckner is a ruin.

119
00:05:24,991 --> 00:05:28,028
The windows are gone,
there is asbestos inside,

120
00:05:28,091 --> 00:05:29,616
and the city fenced it off.

121
00:05:29,778 --> 00:05:31,816
So I looked at it from outside the fence,

122
00:05:31,866 --> 00:05:34,104
which is the only sensible way to see it.

123
00:05:34,266 --> 00:05:36,291
I imagined the bowling alley anyway.

124
00:05:38,021 --> 00:05:40,220
Then I made the mistake
of stepping outside.

125
00:05:40,395 --> 00:05:42,508
One snowflake landed on my nose.

126
00:05:42,683 --> 00:05:43,983
Then its family arrived.

127
00:05:44,158 --> 00:05:45,120
Then the neighbours.

128
00:05:45,296 --> 00:05:49,471
By March, the snow on the ground here
averages about forty-seven inches,

129
00:05:49,521 --> 00:05:51,470
roughly one point two metres.

130
00:05:51,646 --> 00:05:52,820
I am shorter than that.

131
00:05:52,983 --> 00:05:57,033
Over a whole year, Whittier gets
somewhere around twenty feet of snow,

132
00:05:57,146 --> 00:06:01,846
about six metres, and the official tables
don't quite agree on the exact figure.

133
00:06:02,008 --> 00:06:06,133
Count the rain as well and you get
roughly five metres of water a year,

134
00:06:06,258 --> 00:06:07,558
about sixteen feet.

135
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:12,008
If none of it drained away, it would
stand about as tall as a two-storey house.

136
00:06:13,445 --> 00:06:14,494
The wind joined in.

137
00:06:14,657 --> 00:06:17,132
It averages ten to fifteen miles an hour.

138
00:06:17,307 --> 00:06:21,357
But the state's tunnel weather page
says it often blows forty to sixty,

139
00:06:21,469 --> 00:06:24,119
with waves of four to six
feet out in the inlet.

140
00:06:24,282 --> 00:06:26,257
It rolled me back to the front door,

141
00:06:26,307 --> 00:06:28,045
gently, like a bouncer.

142
00:06:28,232 --> 00:06:29,369
Here's what surprised me.

143
00:06:29,532 --> 00:06:30,669
It isn't that cold.

144
00:06:30,844 --> 00:06:34,945
According to the weather records, January
highs hover right around freezing.

145
00:06:35,107 --> 00:06:37,494
My first trip was colder
than this by a lot.

146
00:06:37,669 --> 00:06:40,632
The problem is that everything
is wet, all the time.

147
00:06:40,794 --> 00:06:42,495
My raincoat needed a raincoat.

148
00:06:42,669 --> 00:06:45,282
Back inside, dripping
on the hallway floor,

149
00:06:45,332 --> 00:06:46,520
I changed my plan.

150
00:06:46,682 --> 00:06:49,207
I had come to move here
and take long walks.

151
00:06:49,370 --> 00:06:52,270
New plan: move here and never go outside.

152
00:06:52,444 --> 00:06:55,632
Suddenly, a town in one
building did not look odd.

153
00:06:55,794 --> 00:06:57,457
It looked like the sensible answer.

154
00:07:00,688 --> 00:07:02,163
I want to tell this part plainly.

155
00:07:02,326 --> 00:07:05,263
On March the twenty-seventh,
nineteen sixty-four,

156
00:07:05,413 --> 00:07:07,888
a great earthquake struck southern Alaska.

157
00:07:08,051 --> 00:07:12,026
In Whittier, the quake and the waves
that followed killed thirteen people.

158
00:07:12,188 --> 00:07:15,138
At the time, only about
seventy people lived here.

159
00:07:15,313 --> 00:07:17,300
The waterfront was badly damaged.

160
00:07:17,463 --> 00:07:21,263
Today the port still works, and the
town still stands where it stood.

161
00:07:21,425 --> 00:07:24,576
I stayed at the water for a while
and did not try to be clever.

162
00:07:27,808 --> 00:07:29,045
I went back in July.

163
00:07:29,208 --> 00:07:30,045
Same tunnel.

164
00:07:30,208 --> 00:07:31,396
Different personality.

165
00:07:31,558 --> 00:07:36,396
In summer it opens at five thirty in the
morning and stays open until eleven fifteen

166
00:07:36,446 --> 00:07:37,246
at night.

167
00:07:37,333 --> 00:07:40,246
I arrived one minute
before closing, on purpose,

168
00:07:40,296 --> 00:07:41,621
and drove straight through.

169
00:07:41,783 --> 00:07:45,821
On the far side, the snow was gone
and the mountains had turned green.

170
00:07:45,983 --> 00:07:47,470
Cruise ships tie up here now.

171
00:07:47,646 --> 00:07:50,670
Boats head out into Prince
William Sound to see glaciers.

172
00:07:50,845 --> 00:07:53,258
People go fishing for halibut and salmon,

173
00:07:53,308 --> 00:07:55,220
or paddle kayaks along the shore.

174
00:07:55,383 --> 00:07:59,183
The quiet winter town had turned into
a front door for the whole sound.

175
00:07:59,358 --> 00:08:01,633
The building was still there, of course,

176
00:08:01,683 --> 00:08:02,871
at the foot of the mountain.

177
00:08:03,045 --> 00:08:05,033
But now people were actually outside it,

178
00:08:05,083 --> 00:08:07,070
on purpose, in T-shirts.

179
00:08:07,246 --> 00:08:09,646
I put my slippers away and found my boots.

180
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:10,896
They looked nervous.

181
00:08:12,228 --> 00:08:15,178
On my last day I hiked
the Portage Pass trail,

182
00:08:15,248 --> 00:08:17,498
about two miles out toward Portage Lake,

183
00:08:17,588 --> 00:08:19,598
along the old route over the mountains.

184
00:08:20,028 --> 00:08:21,978
I wore my raincoat, just in case.

185
00:08:22,348 --> 00:08:23,298
It did not rain.

186
00:08:23,468 --> 00:08:24,358
I felt cheated.

187
00:08:24,668 --> 00:08:27,058
Back in town, I learned about a new title.

188
00:08:27,348 --> 00:08:31,698
In October twenty twenty-six, the National
Park Service named Whittier an American

189
00:08:31,748 --> 00:08:33,878
World War Two Heritage City.

190
00:08:34,068 --> 00:08:35,878
Only one city per state gets it.

191
00:08:36,048 --> 00:08:38,298
The town is proud of
the port that made it.

192
00:08:38,488 --> 00:08:41,018
And standing in the sun,
I changed my mind again.

193
00:08:41,068 --> 00:08:42,298
Not forever.

194
00:08:42,588 --> 00:08:43,498
Just for the summer.

195
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:49,190
The back of the timetable was blank,
so I drew the whole story on it.

196
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:53,890
A war needed a port that ships could
reach all year and planes could not see.

197
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:55,010
The fog hid it.

198
00:08:55,160 --> 00:08:56,190
A tunnel reached it.

199
00:08:56,380 --> 00:08:59,090
The Army left, and its
building became the town.

200
00:08:59,340 --> 00:09:03,070
And the weather makes living under
one roof sensible instead of strange.

201
00:09:03,460 --> 00:09:04,270
So, do I move in?

202
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:05,530
Summer only.

203
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:06,910
From May to September,

204
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:09,590
the tunnel runs until
a quarter past eleven,

205
00:09:09,740 --> 00:09:12,230
and Whittier is a doorway to glaciers,

206
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:13,670
fish and trails.

207
00:09:13,980 --> 00:09:16,910
In winter it means roughly
twenty feet of snow,

208
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:18,730
wind that rolls me into doors,

209
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:20,250
and a road with a bedtime.

210
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:21,480
Bring a calendar.

211
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:22,580
Bring patience.

212
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:25,030
Leave the habit of running
late somewhere else.

213
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:27,250
I taped the timetable to my dashboard.

214
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:28,670
This time I read it.

215
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:31,770
Could you spend a whole winter
without stepping out of your building?

216
00:09:33,793 --> 00:09:35,856
The timetable lives on my shelf now,

217
00:09:36,068 --> 00:09:38,443
next to a jar of almost no opals.

218
00:09:38,618 --> 00:09:40,543
The fish still lives in the freezer.

219
00:09:40,705 --> 00:09:42,343
The map has more pins to go.

220
00:09:42,518 --> 00:09:46,143
The next one is warm, very
small, and already full.

221
00:09:46,305 --> 00:09:47,455
I am packing light.
